## What was corrected

Each item below is a statement in v1.0 that v1.1 changes, and the number you should use.

### 1. v1.0 evaluated {{v1.n_images}} of {{n_images}} images

The submission used a seeded, stratified subset to stay reproducible on a laptop. Its confidence intervals therefore describe {{v1.n_test}} test images. v1.1 evaluates all {{n_images}} images; the expert-label test set is {{n_test}} images from {{n_test_sessions}} sessions ({{n_test_bad}} expert-bad). Use the v1.1 intervals.

### 2. The pre-registered reference flagged {{v1.clean_flag}} of clean test images

In v1.0 this was reported as an honest failure (cross-session acquisition shift) with a post-hoc batch reference that lowered it to {{v1.batch_clean_flag}}. v1.1 replaces the single z threshold with per-metric calibrated cutoffs and a session-relative reference. At that operating point {{e1.batch.clean}} (session-relative) and {{e1.between.clean}} (single image) of clean images are flagged. The retired v1.0 rule is not hidden: on v1.1's held-out sessions the pre-registered z={{prereg_z}} rule still flags {{op.pooled.z3}} (pooled reference), {{op.between.z3}} (between-session) and {{op.batch.z3}} (batch) of expert-good images. Detection therefore looks lower in v1.1 ({{e1.batch.det3}} against {{v1.det3}}): that is the honest operating point, not a regression. At a threshold of {{e1.sweep_lo.t}} the same detector finds {{e1.sweep_lo.det}} at {{e1.sweep_lo.clean}} clean false flags (`threshold_sweep`).

### 3. The artifact score's AUROC of {{v1.qc_auroc}} on {{v1.n_test}} images

v1.0 reported {{v1.qc_auroc}} {{v1.qc_ci}} and concluded that the artifact score does not predict the expert label. In v1.1 the score is approximately at chance, with intervals that include chance level: {{e2.qc_batch.auroc}} {{e2.qc_batch.ci}} (session-relative), {{e2.qc_between.auroc}} {{e2.qc_between.ci}} (single image) and {{e2.qc_pooled.auroc}} {{e2.qc_pooled.ci}} (pooled reference); random is {{e2.random.auroc}}. The qualitative conclusion stands; the number is superseded and the apparent below-chance result should not be quoted.

### 4. Cause attribution of {{v1.attr}} among flagged is not comparable with v1.1

The flagged set differs: v1.0 attributed causes for images flagged at a threshold that also flagged {{v1.clean_flag}} of clean images, v1.1 for images flagged at a calibrated threshold, so the two accuracies are computed on different images. In v1.1 the accuracy among flagged faults is {{e1.batch.sig}} with signatures and {{e1.batch.rules}} with the v1.0 rule table (session-relative), {{e1.between.sig}} and {{e1.between.rules}} for single images, against {{e1.chance}} by chance. Treat the v1.0 figure as a measurement of v1.0's flagged set only.

## What did not change

- The grounding gate, the retrieval stack and the corpus. E4 and E5 were re-run offline from the committed caches: their headline values are {{e45_status}} ({{gate.caught}} fabrications caught, {{gate.false_block}} false blocks, unsupported claims {{e5.unsup_un}} to {{e5.unsup_g}}, paper-level MRR {{e4.mrr}}).
- The research-only scope: no clinical or diagnostic claims, certificates are content hashes and not signatures.
- Tag `v1.0-submission`, `docs/WRITEUP.md` and `docs/LIMITATIONS.md`.

## New limitations in v1.1

These are in addition to everything in `docs/LIMITATIONS.md`.

1. **Single-image absolute mode cannot meet the false-alarm target on the upper fifth of unseen sessions.** Leave-one-session-out mean false-flag rate is {{cal.between.loso}} (pooled reference {{cal.pooled.loso}}) against a {{target_ff}} target, and the 80th-percentile session is at {{cal.between.p80}} (pooled {{cal.pooled.p80}}); session-relative scoring is at {{cal.batch.loso}} (80th percentile {{cal.batch.p80}}) but needs a batch of images from the same session ({{api.triage_min}} to {{api.triage_max}} in the API).
2. **The benchmarks are small.** {{n_base}} base images for the injected-fault benchmark, {{vlm.n}} per group for the vision-LLM baseline, {{e5.n_cases}} grounding cases ({{e5.n_adv}} adversarial). Intervals are reported and are wide.
3. **Some fault kinds are mostly missed.** At severity {{sev.mid}} (session-relative): {{miss_list_detail}}. Cause naming for these kinds is also weak.
4. **The culture-state kNN uses expert-labelled neighbours.** It is a lookup against a human judgement of unknown criteria, and in the product the bank includes all {{n_images}} images, so an image from a session already in the bank looks better than one from a new session.
5. **The injected faults are synthetic and ours.** The benchmark shows sensitivity to these faults, not their prevalence or realism.
6. **The head-to-head and the vision-LLM baseline are narrow.** One approach implemented here; one prompt; {{vlm.n_parsed}} models with usable replies and one with no parsable reply on {{vlm.unparsed_cases}} cases.
7. **{{rc.n_unsupported}} of {{n_rules}} lab actions have no verified literature basis** in the {{e4.n_papers}}-paper corpus, and the {{rc.theta}} support threshold was chosen after reading the matches.
8. **The latency figure was measured in the v1.0 run** on {{e9.n}} images and was not re-measured on all {{n_images}}.
9. **One model in the LLM sweep was not measured**: the Cohere chat model, because the shared trial key reached its monthly quota.

## Post-hoc choices, disclosed

| Choice | When | Where it is recorded |
|---|---|---|
| Batch (session-relative) reference | After v1.0's cross-session result | `docs/WRITEUP.md` section 6 (v1.0) |
| Per-metric calibrated cutoffs and signature attribution | After v1.0's failures; fitted on development sessions only | `results/calibration.json`, `scripts/eval_vision_v2.py` |
| Rule-citation threshold {{rc.theta_default}} to {{rc.theta}} | After reading the matches | `results/rule_citations.json` → `disclosure` |
| Reasoning-model settings in the LLM sweep | One probe per model | `docs/LLM_SWEEP.md` |

No threshold was tuned on test sessions in v1.1. The designer had seen v1.0's test results before designing v1.1, and the split was redrawn at session level, so a prospective validation with a partner lab remains the only clean test.

## Reproduce

```bash
git checkout next
make judge                                   # tests, retrieval and grounding replay, provenance check; no keys
python evaluate.py                           # all results (needs cache/features_all.pkl from `make features`)
python scripts/build_defense_kit.py          # regenerates the deck, Q&A, talk track and this file
```
